   "summary": "The article 'Horizontal gene transfer in eukaryotes: aligning theory with data' published in *Nature Reviews Genetics* (Volume 25, Pages 416-430, 2024) discusses the role of horizontal gene transfer (HGT) in eukaryotic evolution. While HGT is well-documented in bacteria and archaea, its significance in eukaryotes is less clear. Eukaryotic behaviors such as phagocytosis and endosymbiosis are hypothesized to increase HGT frequency, but eukaryotic nuclear genomes contain fewer HGTs compared to prokaryotes. The review examines the existing theoretical framework and empirical data, suggesting that the reduced need for new genes in eukaryotes, due to effective selection on variation, offsets the potential benefits of increased HGT frequency. Key studies and debates are highlighted, including the controversy over the extent of HGT in eukaryotic nuclear genomes and the impact of HGT on eukaryotic cellular complexity and adaptive evolution.",
   "key_excerpts": "It has played a pronounced part in bacterial and archaeal evolution, but its role in eukaryotes is less clear., Behaviours unique to eukaryotic cells — phagocytosis and endosymbiosis — have been proposed to increase the frequency of HGT, but nuclear genomes encode fewer HGTs than bacteria and archaea., any increased chance of acquiring new genes through phagocytosis and endosymbiosis is offset by a reduced need for these genes in eukaryotes, because selection in most eukaryotes operates on variation not readily generated by HGT., This Review aims to turn the page on the debate from whether HGT affected eukaryotes to focusing on how it changed their ecology and evolution., The physiology of phagocytosis in the context of mitochondrial origin is discussed, emphasizing the importance of functional HGT in evolutionary persistence."
